diff --git a/scala/src/uciedigital/logphy/modules/MainbandRxAligner.scala b/scala/src/uciedigital/logphy/modules/MainbandRxAligner.scala
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..32f5e35
--- /dev/null
+++ b/scala/src/uciedigital/logphy/modules/MainbandRxAligner.scala
@@ -0,0 +1,205 @@
+/*
+  Description:
+    Aligns the mainband receive word to the partner's flit boundary.
+
+    WHY THIS IS NEEDED AT ALL
+
+    The analog receive tile hands the digital layer a word on a boundary that
+    has nothing to do with the partner. resources/vsrc/rx_data_lane.v shifts
+    every UI into a 32-bit register and latches it out whenever its own 3-bit
+    counter wraps; that counter is cleared by this die's reset and free-runs
+    from there. So which of the 32 UI in a delivered word is the partner's UI 0
+    is decided by this die's reset time, not by anything on the wire. The tile
+    says so itself:
+
+        "Keeping this sequential is also what lets the digital RX realign on an
+         arbitrary word boundary, since an offset capture is then a rotation of
+         the stream rather than a scramble."
+
+    That realignment was never built. Its absence is already on record
+    elsewhere in the stack: the MBINIT pattern comparisons had to be made
+    tolerant of any rotation of the expected pattern, against measured offsets
+    of 9 UI in CLKREPAIR, 2 UI in VALTRAIN and 6 UI in PERLANEID (see
+    PatternReader's clkRepairRotations). Rotation tolerance is a workable answer for a
+    fixed training pattern, where the whole word is known in advance. It is no
+    answer at all for mission data, where the payload is exactly what is not
+    known, so a misframed word is delivered as garbage rather than rejected.
+
+    The offset is also not a constant of the link, and that was measured rather
+    than inferred. Reading the raw valid word at the analog boundary, ahead of
+    this module, shows two regimes depending on how busy the link is:
+
+      contiguous   the partner's flits run back to back, raw valid reads
+                   0x0f0f0f0f word after word, and one offset serves the whole
+                   stream. Framing is measured once. This is the lucky case.
+      spaced       the partner's flits are separated by idle, and each starts
+                   at a phase unrelated to the last. Raw valid reads
+                   0x0f0f0000, 0x0f0f0f0f, 0x3c3c0000, 0xc3c3c3c0, 0xf0000000,
+                   0xf0f00000 ... -- offsets of 16, 0, 18, 6, 28 and 20 UI on
+                   consecutive flits. Framing was measured 256 times in one run
+                   and gave a different answer 239 of them.
+
+    Both regimes appear in the same testbench, chosen by nothing more than how
+    hard the other die happens to be transmitting. So the offset cannot be a
+    parameter, and cannot be latched once and trusted: anything that latches
+    once is right in the first regime and wrong on every flit after the first
+    in the second.
+
+    DEPENDENCY ON THE LANE SHUFFLER
+
+    This works on the word as it comes past the per-lane shuffler, where bit i
+    is UI i. The RX tile itself does not deliver that: it deserializes through
+    an adjacent-pairing tree, so dout(j) is the bit received in UI bitrev5(j),
+    and the shuffler's reset value (Phy.treeBitOrder, set in TileLink.scala)
+    cancels it. Shifting is only a rotation once that has happened -- in tree
+    order an offset capture is a scramble, and this module would be meaningless
+    ahead of the shuffler or with it programmed to identity.
+
+    HOW IT IS MEASURED
+
+    The valid lane is the reference, and it needs no negotiation. It is held
+    low between flits and carries the framing pattern -- four UI asserted, four
+    idle -- while a flit is on the wire, so the first asserted UI after an idle
+    stretch is the partner's UI 0. Alignment is therefore established on the
+    first non-idle word that follows a fully idle one, and re-established every
+    time that happens, which covers a partner restarting a burst at a different
+    phase, a retrain, and a different partner entirely.
+
+    Re-arming only after a fully idle word is what makes it safe: the last word
+    of a burst is also a partial word, and taking its first asserted UI as a
+    new offset would corrupt the alignment just as the burst ended.
+
+    This assumes a partner that holds the valid lane low between flits, which
+    the framing pattern requires. A partner that asserted valid continuously
+    from the moment it entered ACTIVE would give no idle word to anchor on, and
+    the valid pattern alone cannot resolve which of the four 8-UI groups in the
+    word is the first -- that case is not handled.
+
+    Measured against the partner here: its first flit began 16 UI into this
+    die's receive word, and every following word carried UI 16..31 of one flit
+    followed by UI 0..15 of the next. Once shifted by 16 the received words
+    matched the partner's scrambling LFSR stream bit for bit, which is what
+    identified the offset as framing rather than corruption.
+ */
+
+package edu.berkeley.cs.uciedigital.logphy
+
+import chisel3._
+import chisel3.util._
+
+class MainbandRxAlignerIO(afeParams: AfeParams) extends Bundle {
+  val in = Flipped(
+    Valid(new MainbandLanes(afeParams.mbLanes, afeParams.mbSerializerRatio))
+  )
+  val out = Valid(
+    new MainbandLanes(afeParams.mbLanes, afeParams.mbSerializerRatio)
+  )
+
+  /** The aligned word carries flit data rather than idle. The descrambler
+    * advances on this, so that it starts at its seed on the partner's first
+    * flit word exactly as the transmit scrambler does.
+    */
+  val dataPresent = Output(Bool())
+
+  /** Force a re-arm, on returning to ACTIVE. Alignment also re-establishes
+    * itself at every idle-to-data transition, so this is belt and braces.
+    */
+  val restart = Input(Bool())
+
+  val aligned = Output(Bool())
+  val offset = Output(UInt(log2Ceil(afeParams.mbSerializerRatio).W))
+
+  /** How many times framing was measured, and how many of those produced a
+    * different answer from the one being held. The second is the number that
+    * decides whether re-measuring is required or merely harmless: if the
+    * offset never moves, latching once would do; if it moves, anything that
+    * latches once is wrong on every burst after the first.
+    */
+  val rearms = Output(UInt(16.W))
+  val offsetChanges = Output(UInt(16.W))
+}
+
+class MainbandRxAligner(afeParams: AfeParams) extends Module {
+  val io = IO(new MainbandRxAlignerIO(afeParams))
+
+  val serRatio = afeParams.mbSerializerRatio
+  val nLanes = afeParams.mbLanes
+
+  val prev = Reg(new MainbandLanes(nLanes, serRatio))
+  val havePrev = RegInit(false.B)
+  val alignOffset = RegInit(0.U(log2Ceil(serRatio).W))
+  val aligned = RegInit(false.B)
+
+  private val framePattern = Seq.tabulate(serRatio)(i => (i % 8) < 4)
+
+  private val maxIntraFlitIdle = framePattern
+    .foldLeft((0, 0)) { case ((run, mx), on) =>
+      if (on) (0, mx) else (run + 1, math.max(mx, run + 1))
+    }
+    ._2
+  private val burstGapUi = maxIntraFlitIdle + 1
+
+
+  val idleRun = RegInit(serRatio.U(8.W))
+  val firstSet = PriorityEncoder(io.in.bits.valid)
+  val trailingIdle = PriorityEncoder(Reverse(io.in.bits.valid))
+  val wordIdle = io.in.bits.valid === 0.U
+
+  when(io.in.valid) {
+    idleRun := Mux(
+      wordIdle,
+      Mux(idleRun > (255 - serRatio).U, 255.U, idleRun + serRatio.U),
+      trailingIdle
+    )
+  }
+
+  val idleBefore = idleRun +& firstSet
+  val burstStart = io.in.valid && !wordIdle && (idleBefore >= burstGapUi.U)
+  val measured = firstSet
+
+  val effOffset = Mux(burstStart, measured, alignOffset)
+
+  private def shift(older: UInt, newer: UInt): UInt =
+    (Cat(newer, older) >> effOffset)(serRatio - 1, 0)
+
+  val rearmCount = RegInit(0.U(16.W))
+  val offsetChangeCount = RegInit(0.U(16.W))
+
+  when(burstStart) {
+    alignOffset := measured
+    aligned := true.B
+    rearmCount := rearmCount + 1.U
+    when(aligned && measured =/= alignOffset) {
+      offsetChangeCount := offsetChangeCount + 1.U
+    }
+  }
+
+  when(io.in.valid) {
+    prev := io.in.bits
+    havePrev := true.B
+  }
+
+  when(io.restart) {
+    havePrev := false.B
+    aligned := false.B
+    alignOffset := 0.U
+    idleRun := serRatio.U
+  }
+
+  val alignedValid = shift(prev.valid, io.in.bits.valid)
+
+  io.out.valid := io.in.valid && havePrev
+  io.out.bits.valid := alignedValid
+  io.out.bits.clkP := shift(prev.clkP, io.in.bits.clkP)
+  io.out.bits.clkN := shift(prev.clkN, io.in.bits.clkN)
+  io.out.bits.trk := shift(prev.trk, io.in.bits.trk)
+  for (lane <- 0 until nLanes) {
+    io.out.bits.data(lane) := shift(prev.data(lane), io.in.bits.data(lane))
+  }
+
+  io.dataPresent := io.out.valid && (alignedValid =/= 0.U)
+  io.aligned := aligned
+  io.offset := alignOffset
+  io.rearms := rearmCount
+  io.offsetChanges := offsetChangeCount
+}
